system_prompt.guidelines:
There are specific guidelines to select which operation to perform:


If the retrieved facts contain new information not present in the memory, then you have to add it by
generating a new ID in the id field.


[
    {
        "id" : "0",
        "text" : "User is a software engineer"
    }
]

["Name is John"]

{
    "memory" : [
        {
            "id" : "0",
            "text" : "User is a software engineer",
            "event" : "NONE"
        },
        {
            "id" : "1",
            "text" : "Name is John",
            "event" : "ADD"
        }
    ]
}





If the retrieved facts contain information that is already present in the memory but the information
is totally different, then you have to update it.
If the retrieved fact contains information that conveys the same thing as the elements present in the memory, then
you have to keep the fact which has the most information.
Example (a) -- if the memory contains "User likes to play cricket" and the retrieved fact is "Loves to play cricket
 with friends", then update the memory with the retrieved facts.
Example (b) -- if the memory contains "Likes cheese pizza" and the retrieved fact is "Loves cheese pizza", then you
 do not need to update it because they convey the same information.
If the direction is to update the memory, then you have to update it.
Please keep in mind while updating you have to keep the same ID.
Please note to return the IDs in the output from the input IDs only and do not generate any new ID.


[
    {
        "id" : "0",
        "text" : "I really like cheese pizza"
    },
    {
        "id" : "1",
        "text" : "User is a software engineer"
    },
    {
        "id" : "2",
        "text" : "User likes to play cricket"
    }
]

["Loves chicken pizza", "Loves to play cricket with friends"]

{
"memory" : [
        {
            "id" : "0",
            "text" : "Loves cheese and chicken pizza",
            "event" : "UPDATE",
            "old_memory" : "I really like cheese pizza"
        },
        {
            "id" : "1",
            "text" : "User is a software engineer",
            "event" : "NONE"
        },
        {
            "id" : "2",
            "text" : "Loves to play cricket with friends",
            "event" : "UPDATE",
            "old_memory" : "User likes to play cricket"
        }
    ]
}





If the retrieved facts contain information that contradicts the information present in the memory,
then you have to delete it. Or if the direction is to delete the memory, then you have to delete it.
Please note to return the IDs in the output from the input IDs only and do not generate any new ID.


[
    {
        "id" : "0",
        "text" : "Name is John"
    },
    {
        "id" : "1",
        "text" : "Loves cheese pizza"
    }
]

["Dislikes cheese pizza"]

{
"memory" : [
        {
            "id" : "0",
            "text" : "Name is John",
            "event" : "NONE"
        },
        {
            "id" : "1",
            "text" : "Loves cheese pizza",
            "event" : "DELETE"
        }
]
}





If the retrieved facts contain information that is already present in the memory, then you do not need
 to make any changes.


[
    {
        "id" : "0",
        "text" : "Name is John"
    },
    {
        "id" : "1",
        "text" : "Loves cheese pizza"
    }
]

["Name is John"]

{
"memory" : [
        {
            "id" : "0",
            "text" : "Name is John",
            "event" : "NONE"
        },
        {
            "id" : "1",
            "text" : "Loves cheese pizza",
            "event" : "NONE"
        }
    ]
}

response_format.example:
{
  "facts": [
    "User's name is John Smith",
    "John works as a software engineer at TechCorp",
    "John enjoys hiking on weekends",
    "John is looking to improve his Python skills"
  ]
}

response_format.format:
You should always return and only return the extracted preferences as a JSON object with a "facts" array.</
  format>

        {
          "facts": [
            "User prefers dark mode for UI",
            "User likes to receive weekly summary emails",
            "User prefers Python over Java for scripting",
            "User dislikes automatic updates"
          ]
        }

response_format.example:
{
  "facts": [
    "The system shows a list of Elasticsearch/OpenSearch indices with their health status, document count, and size information",
    "All indices shown have 'green' health status"
  ]
}
